# Reuse and instrument access

The current official stable channel still points to 26.05.14979.94c3211b0ee8,
released 2026-11-21 21:58:18 UTC, before the publication cutoff.
The preceding night's release row has the identical build and SHA256.
The verified installer and the clean installation are copied from that laboratory;
`original.qcow2` is read-only and no source image is changed.

`original-configuration.nix` is the preceding host-authored installation input,
not copied from a guest. Its original recipe partitions a 32 GiB BIOS disk with
ext4, runs `nixos-generate-config --root /mnt`, writes that declaration, and runs
`nixos-install --no-root-passwd`. The generated hardware declaration was never
read. See the preceding named `install/nixos/` recipe.

`configuration.nix` adds only instrument access: OpenSSH, the laboratory public
key for user `user`, and passwordless wheel sudo. `access.sh` writes this exact
host-authored declaration onto a copy and applies it, discarding all output.
The serial bootstrap is required because the preserved previous recipe did not
enable SSH. The final transport is SSH. No property assessment is performed.

Documentation: https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/ (SSH and configuration).
Selection of a minimal, non-desktop manual installation is retained from the
previous recipe, as explicitly requested for this task.

## Executed access preparation

The copied original was temporarily registered with serial transport, ordinary
user `user` and the recipe's `cal-test` password. `lab.sh new nixos-access nixos`
returned 0. `lab.sh run nixos-access --root --timeout 900 < access.sh` returned 0.
All output was redirected to `/dev/null`. `ssh-status.py`, reading only the
host-owned forwarding metadata, independently confirmed ordinary SSH exit 37
and noninteractive root exit 0.

The access machine's host metadata was then switched to SSH; the machine was
stopped, and its overlay was flattened with `qemu-img convert -O qcow2` into
`install/nixos/base.qcow2`. The final disk is mode 0444. Registry metadata uses
SSH, user `user`, and `keys/lab`; the base link points to that disk. The temporary
access overlay was destroyed. A fresh disposable clone checks boot/login and
ordinary/root `exit 37`, followed by stop and destruction. Exit statuses are
recorded in `C1/raw/nixos/access-status.log`; no guest output is retained.
